<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Tehri Garhwal State</h1><p class="st-meta">1815 CE – 1949 CE · capital Tehri</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Himalayan kingdom of the Garhwal dynasty, restored by the British in 1815 after Gorkha occupation and ruled from Tehri until 1949.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parmar-rajputs">Panwar</a> kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garhwal">Garhwal</a> had ruled the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> hills from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srinagar-uttarakhand">Srinagar</a> since medieval times. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gurkhas">Gorkhas</a> conquered Garhwal in 1804. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-nepalese-war">Anglo-Nepalese War</a>, the British in 1815 restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudarshan-shah">Sudarshan Shah</a> to the western part of his kingdom, keeping the east as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-garhwal">British Garhwal</a>; he founded a new capital at Tehri.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Tehri <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garhwal">Garhwal</a> was a mountain state of terraced farms, forests and pilgrimage routes to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gangotri">Gangotri</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamunotri">Yamunotri</a>. Its rulers profited from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and controlled access to forest lands, provoking peasant protests such as the one crushed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tilari-massacre">Tilari</a> in 1930. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narendra-shah">Narendra Shah</a> founded the new capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narendranagar">Narendranagar</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After popular agitation ended the monarchy's authority, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manabendra-shah">Manabendra Shah</a> acceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and Tehri <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garhwal">Garhwal</a> was merged into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a> in 1949. It is now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttarakhand">Uttarakhand</a>; old Tehri town was submerged by the Tehri Dam.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/tehri-garhwal-state.jpg" alt="Tehri Garhwal State" width="320" height="215"><figcaption>Image: Robert Alfers · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Princely_State_of_Tehri_Garhwal.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1815 CE – 1949 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tehri</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sudarshan Shah, Narendra Shah, Manabendra Shah</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Garhwali</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Tehri Garhwal, Tehri</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1882&amp;focus=tehri-garhwal-state" aria-label="Tehri Garhwal State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6716 1069 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Tehri Garhwal State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7036 1325L7036 1325M7036 1325L7364 1601M7036 1325L7364 1602M7036 1325L7018 1386"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7036 1325h0M7364 1601h0M7364 1602h0M7018 1386h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7036 1325h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7036 1325h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1882&amp;focus=tehri-garhwal-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
